// The linked list, which is the first thing anybody tries, and it CRASHED the compiler.
//
//     class Node { label: String, next: Maybe<Node> }
//
// A `Node` holds a `Maybe<Node>` by value, which holds a `Node` by value. No finite size — and
// stage-0 answered "no errors", then died with a bare `stack overflow, aborting` and a core dump
// in `build` and in `layout`. An anonymous stack overflow is the one failure this language forbids,
// and the compiler was the thing doing it.
//
// The finiteness walk existed and followed only `Type::Named` FIELDS, so it stepped from `Node`
// into the instantiation and stopped there, having found no struct fields — because an
// instantiated enum has variants instead. One arm, not a new rule.
//
// **What must stay legal is the reason this is not a blunt "no self-reference" ban**, and all
// three of these compile today:
//
//     class Node { kids: [Node] }                   a slice is a pointer
//     class Node { kids: Map<String, Node> }        a generic whose fields are slices
//     enum Tree { Branch([Tree]), Leaf(Int) }       most recursive data, written this way
//
// So the rule is FINITE WIDTH and a slice ends the walk. An array does not: it is N copies.
enum Maybe<T> { Just(T), Nothing }

class Node { label: String, next: Maybe<Node> }

let x: Int = 1;
print(x);
